A new free tool makes a MacBook's Force Touch trackpad produce speech and music by vibrating its glass, even when the computer's speakers are muted.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/macbook-pro-keyboard-black-bg.jpg" alt="" width="5120" height="2880" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1039960" /><br/>
The open-source project is called "<a href="https://github.com/os6/nospeaker">NO SPEAKER</a>," and comes from developer Cody Collier, who recently shared shared a demonstration on X. The tool uses the small haptic motor that normally makes a Force Touch trackpad feel as though it clicks. Driving that motor at different frequencies makes the glass vibrate fast enough to produce audible sounds.<br/>
<br/>
The demo lasts around a minute and a half and includes speech, a melody, a beat, and a short rap. Users can also slide a finger across the trackpad to change the pitch of a tone, enter their own text for it to speak, or choose another installed macOS voice. The tool does not turn the trackpad into a normal audio output for playing music files or sound from other apps.<br/>
