To generate speech, the software starts with a voice created by macOS's built-in text-to-speech feature. It breaks the recording into tiny sections and picks out the main sound frequencies that make the words recognizable. The motor then reproduces a simplified version of those sounds through the trackpad.<br/>
<br/>
The result is a quiet, robotic voice. Collier says the words are easier to follow when reading the text displayed alongside them, and recommends placing the MacBook on a table to make the sound easier to hear.<br/>
<br/>
The tool uses parts of macOS that Apple does not make available for third-party developers to use, namely the "MultitouchSupport" framework. These let it control how fast and strongly the trackpad vibrates, rather than simply trigger a preset click. Since Apple does not officially support this use-case, a future macOS update could stop the tool from working.<br/>
<br/>
Collier says the tool keeps the haptic motor within its normal maximum strength, but it is unknown whether prolonged use could affect the trackpad's lifespan. NO SPEAKER requires a Force Touch trackpad and macOS Monterey or later on Apple silicon, or macOS Ventura or later on Intel. The tool is free and <a href="https://github.com/os6/nospeaker/releases/tag/v1.0">available now on GitHub</a>.<div class="linkback">Tag: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/magic-trackpad/">Magic Trackpad</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/23/macbook-trackpad-turns-into-a-speaker/">Developer Turns MacBook Trackpad Into a Speaker Using Haptic Motor</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/developer-turns-macbook-trackpad-into-a-speaker-using-haptic-motor.2495065/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>